   "text": "This SMILES was read from the drawing region by region. I could not count the carbamate linker's methylene chain with full certainty, so that part is the least reliable.\n\n**What the drawing shows**\n- **Peptide:** an N-acetylated peptide, read from the N-terminus: Ac-Cys-Pro-Pro-Hyp(4-OH)-Asp-Ala-Tyr-Leu-Gly-Cys-tBuGly-Ser-Tyr-Cys-Glu-Pro-Trp-Lys-NH2.\n- **Hydroxyproline:** the drawing gives the Hyp 4-OH a hashed bond; I assigned the (2S,4R) trans-L form.\n- **Scaffold:** the three Cys thiols are each alkylated by a bromoacetyl-type arm. The three arms are carried on the three nitrogens of a 1,3,5-triacyl-hexahydro-1,3,5-triazine, which closes the two peptide loops.\n- **Lysine side chain:** the Lys side chain (four CH2 groups) ends on N1 of a 1,2,3-triazole. The triazole is fused to a bicyclo[6.1.0]nonene ring (BCN, the strained-alkyne click product).\n- **Cyclopropane:** both ring-fusion hydrogens are wedged and the CH2O substituent is hashed. From the wedges I derived the three cyclopropane stereocentres.\n- **Linker:** the CH2O group continues as a carbamate, O\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013(CH2)4\u2013CH(CH2OH)\u2013CH2\u2013O\u2013P(=O)(OH)\u2013, ending in the wavy bond, written as `*`.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- I assigned each wedge or hash by CIP analysis. All the amino acids come out as the natural L-configuration (Cys R, the others S).\n- The Cys residues are R only because of the CH2S priority.\n- tert-Leucine is S.\n\n**SMILES check**\n- Ring-closure digits pair up: the three triazinane arms close onto the three Cys CH2 groups, and the BCN and indole rings close as drawn.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)N[C@@H](CSCC(=O)N1CN(C(=O)CS2)CN(C(=O)CS3)C1)C(=O)N4CCC[C@H]4C(=O)N5C[C@H](O)C[C@H]5C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](C)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc6ccc(O)cc6)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)NCC(=O)N[C@@H](C3)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](CO)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc7ccc(O)cc7)C(=O)N[C@@H](C2)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCC(=O)O)C(=O)N8CCC[C@H]8C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc9c[nH]c%10ccccc9%10)C(=O)N[C@H](C(N)=O)CC